From Gerald.Jean at spgdag.ca  Wed Oct  1 14:18:32 2003
From: Gerald.Jean at spgdag.ca
Date: Wed, 01 Oct 2003 10:18:32 -0400
Subject: Summary and clarification: Cramer's V statistics
Message-ID: <OF1DC16BEA.4C7FFB84-ON85256DB2.004CE5DD@spgdag.ca>

Hello S-users,

thank you very much to Joseph Lucke and to Marc Schwartz for their replies.
I think I didn't voice clearly enough what I was trying to do.  I would
like to calculate Cramer's V statistics, as a measure of association
between categorical variables, for a large number of factors from
moderately large and large data sets.  I have written a little function to
do this and the bottle neck in the process is the calculation of the
counts, those counts are not only the number of observations in each cell
but the sum of a weight variable for each cell.  Following you will find my
little function, the "tapply" in there is consuming all the time.  I know I
could use the builtin "chisq.test" once the counts are calculated but, as
far as I can see, there is no gain in doing so.

Thanks for any suggestions for improvements, BTW it took a little over four
hours to run for a moderately large data set, 175K observations and 49
factor variables on a Solaris 5.8 running a 64-bits version of Splus.

Gérald Jean
Analyste-conseil (statistiques), Actuariat
télephone            : (418) 835-4900 poste (7639)
télecopieur          : (418) 835-6657
courrier électronique: gerald.jean at spgdag.ca

"In God we trust all others must bring data"  W. Edwards Deming

"CramerV" <- function(vnames, data = sys.parent(), weights = NULL)
{
### Author: Gérald Jean
### Date  : 30 Sep 2003, 09:45
### Purpose: Calculate Cramer's V, which is a measure of association between
### categorical variables.
### ----------------------------------------------------------------------
### Arguments:
###  vnames : the variables for which Cramer's V will be calculated.  The
###           variables must be factors.
###  weights: a character string giving the name, if any, of the variable
###           giving the number of units represented by each observation.
###  data   : the data from which to extract the variables and the weights.
### ----------------------------------------------------------------------

### Testing if all variables are categorical.

  tmp.time             <- proc.time()[1:3]
  catvars <- unlist(lapply(vnames, FUN = function(v, data)
                           is.factor(data[, v]), data))
  if (any(!catvars)){
    cat(paste('\n\tThe follwing variables are not factors, they will not be ',
              'considered.', sep = ''), sep = '\n')
    cat(paste('\n\t---> ', vnames[!catvars], sep = ''), sep = '')
    cat(' ', sep = '\n')
  }
  vnames <- vnames[catvars]
  m <- length(vnames)
  if (m == 0) stop('\n\n\tNo categorical variables.\n')
  if (is.null(weights)) weights <- rep(1, nrow(data))
  N <- length(weights)

### Computing all the N_ij, the Eij, the Qp and finally the Cramer's V.

  Vij <- lapply(vnames[1:(m - 1)], FUN =
                       function(main.name, all.name, data, m, w, N)
   {num <- which(all.name == main.name)
    cat(paste('\n\tThe variable ', main.name, ' with:', sep = ''), sep = '')
    one.var.Qp <- lapply(all.name[(num + 1):m], FUN =
           function(one.name, main.name, data, w, N)
     {
      cat(paste('\n\t\t', one.name, sep = ''), sep = '')
      Nij <- tapply(w, IND = data[, c(main.name, one.name)], FUN = sum)
      colsS <- colSums(Nij)
      rowsS <- rowSums(Nij)
      Eij <- (as.matrix(rowsS) %*% colsS) / N
      Qp <- sum(rowSums((Nij - Eij)^2 / Eij))
      sqrt((Qp / N) / min(dim(Nij) - 1))
     }, main.name = main.name, data = data, w = w, N = N)
    names(one.var.Qp) <- all.name[(num + 1):m]
    unlist(one.var.Qp)
   }, all.name = vnames, data = data, m = m, w = weights, N = N)
  names(Vij) <- vnames[1:(m - 1)]

### The list Vij is transformed into a matrix.

  V <- matrix(0, ncol = m, nrow = m)
  for(i in 1:(m - 1)){
    V[i, (i + 1):m] <- Vij[[i]]
  }
  dimnames(V) <- list(vnames, vnames)
  V <- V + t(V)
  diag(V) <- 1
  cat('\n\t ', sep = '\n')
  ttt <- ElapsedTime(tmp.time, proc.time()[1:3])
  V
}

