From spencer.graves at pdf.com  Thu Oct  2 13:34:53 2003
From: spencer.graves at pdf.com (Spencer Graves)
Date: Thu, 02 Oct 2003 06:34:53 -0700
Subject: Re: correlation of coefficients
In-Reply-To: <3F7BD633.FA1D6031@ha.org.hk>
References: <3F7BD633.FA1D6031@ha.org.hk>
Message-ID: <3F7C297D.9010405@pdf.com>

     I suggest you type "lm" at a command prompt and read the code.  
Make a copy in a script file, and add your own comment lines and 
additional variables, which you can then output in a list to see better 
what it is doing;  I did that and learned a lot doing it.  

     Regarding your specific question, consider the following: 

     fit <- lm(..., qr=T)
     R <- qr.R(fit$qr)
     XtX <- (t(R) %*% R)
     XtX.inv <- solve(XtX)
     stdev.b.hat <- sqrt(diag(XtX.inv))
     cor.b.hat <- (XtX.inv / outer(stdev.b.hat, stdev.b.hat))

I have not checked this code, but I've done things like this in the 
past, so some variant should work.  The mathematics behind this is 
explained in any good book that discusses the matrix formulation of 
regression, e.g., Draper and Smith, Applied Regression analysis and Box, 
Hunter, Hunter, Statistics for Experimenters.  I'm sure there are many 
more recent books the discussion this, but I know these two. 

hope this helps. 
spencer graves

