References: <3E3C279AF3F9D411BAA00002A529150E0664A95F@s0-ott-x10.nrn.nrcan.gc.ca>
Message-ID: <Pine.GSO.4.10.10310021451520.20448-100000@tajo.ucsd.edu>

Bob,

On Thu, 2 Oct 2003, Garrett, Robert wrote:

> I have looked in the archives, perhaps not using the correct search strings,
> for a function that undertakes linear clustering as defined by W.D. Fisher,
> 1958, JASA 53(284)789:798.  

Fisher describes several problems in that paper. I believe the
'unrestricted' clustering problem can be dealt with by 'rpart' along these
lines

> y <- rnorm(10) # sample data set
> x <- y #         a copy thereof 
> fit <- rpart( y ~ x, control = rpart.control( minsplit = 2 , cp = 0 ) )
> plot(fit)
> text(fit)
> four.classes <- prune( fit , cp =  fit$cp[ 4, "CP" ] ) # for example
> plot(four.classes)
> text(four.classes)
> predict(four.classes)

Of course, this is not necessarily the optimal partition, which for larger
problems is a combinatorial challenge.

Certain 'restricted' problems can be solved this way. For example, if 'x'
was (instead) a location along a transect and only adjacent members are to
be joined in a stratum, the above code would work.

I believe the 'weights' of rpart correspond to the w_i of Fisher.

'lcluster' (an R package available at http://hacuna.ucsd.edu/lcluster/ )
will treat those two setups using an agglomerative procedure. It has fewer
stops and whistles than rpart, but its algorithm features run times on the
order of N^1.5. Also, it does not allow weights, but these would not be
hard to implement.

Chuck

