From andy_liaw at merck.com  Mon Oct  6 19:50:38 2003
From: andy_liaw at merck.com (Liaw, Andy)
Date: Mon, 06 Oct 2003 15:50:38 -0400
Subject: Re: where is data in cv.tree
In-Reply-To: <20031004175405.GA14183%alexy.khrabrov@setup.org>
Message-ID: <3A822319EB35174CA3714066D590DCD50205CC2E@usrymx25.merck.com>

This is the classic case of the difference in scoping rules between R and
Splus.  In where.is.X(), you defined y, but did not put that into X.  Thus
when it calls best.tree.lost(), it couldn't find y in X, so it starts
looking elsewhere.  That "elsewhere" is where R and Splus differs.  Splus
looks in frame 1 next, then frame 0, then the search path.  R goes up the
chain of where functions are defined until "y" is found, or if not, the
global environment, then the search path.

Try the simple example:

fitlm <- function(n) {
  y <- rnorm(10)
  dat <- data.frame(x=rnorm(n))
  lm(y~x, dat)
}

Calling fitlm(10) in Splus gives you the similar error you saw.  Calling it
in R gives the "right" result.

If you had inserted y into X as follows, you'd have no problem.  That's the
point I was making in my previous message.  If you don't under stand the
scoping rule, don't depend on it.  Put the variables in the data frame
explicitly so that there's no source of confusion as to where they get
looked up.

fit1 <- function(n) fitlm(n)
where.is.X <- function() {
   nx <- 3
   ny <- 4
   y <- as.factor(c(1,0,1,1))
   X <- data.frame(y=y, matrix(c(1:(nx*ny)), nrow = ny, ncol = nx,
                          dimnames=list(c(),paste("x",1:nx,sep=""))))
 
   btr <- best.tree.lost(y~., X)
   print(summary(btr))
}

Andy

