From spencer.graves at pdf.com  Wed Oct  8 16:09:47 2003
From: spencer.graves at pdf.com (Spencer Graves)
Date: Wed, 08 Oct 2003 09:09:47 -0700
Subject: Re: factor levels
In-Reply-To: <sf8438c1.075@Iris.lincoln.landcareresearch.co.nz>
References: <sf8438c1.075@Iris.lincoln.landcareresearch.co.nz>
Message-ID: <3F8436CB.3080309@pdf.com>

     This is discussed, e.g., in Venables and Ripley (2002) Modern 
Applied Statistics with S, 4th ed. (Springer, p. 14): 

> x <- factor(letters[1:5])
> levels(x)
[1] "a" "b" "c" "d" "e"
> levels(x[1:3])
[1] "a" "b" "c" "d" "e"
> x[1:3]
[1] a b c
Levels: a b c d e
> levels(x[1:3, drop=TRUE])
[1] "a" "b" "c"

################

     Unfortunately, this is not so easy with factor columns of a 
"data.frame".  Consider the following: 

> DF <- data.frame(a=letters[1:5], b=LETTERS[1:5])

> sapply(DF, class)

      a        b

"factor" "factor"

> sapply(DF[1:3, , drop=TRUE], levels)

    a   b 
[1,] "a" "A"

[2,] "b" "B"

[3,] "c" "C"

[4,] "d" "D"

[5,] "e" "E"

## Let's try to get rid of the unused levels with "sapply": 

> (DF3s <- as.data.frame(sapply(DF, function(x)x[1:3, drop=TRUE])))
 a b
1 1 1
2 2 2
3 3 3

##   OOPS:  THE "levels" DISAPPEARED. 
> sapply(DF3s, class)

       a         b

"integer" "integer"

## However, if we use "lapply" rather than "sapply", we get what we want: 

> DF3 <- as.data.frame(lapply(DF, function(x)x[1:3, drop=TRUE]))

> sapply(DF3, levels)

    a   b 
[1,] "a" "A"

[2,] "b" "B"

[3,] "c" "C"

## This curious behavior of "sapply" smells like a bug to me.  However, 
it works the same in R 1.7.1 as in S-Plus 6.1, so the bug must just be 
in my head. 

hope this helps. 
spencer graves

