At Wednesday 09:09 AM 10/8/2003 -0700, Spencer Graves wrote:
> [material deleted]
>
> ## This curious behavior of "sapply" smells like a bug to me.  However, it
> works the same in R 1.7.1 as in S-Plus 6.1, so the bug must just be in my
> head.
>
> hope this helps. spencer graves

sapply() is a dangerous beast, in that it will attempt to turn its result 
into a matrix if the result for each element of the original list has the 
same length.  What makes it somewhat dangerous is that it uses unlist() to 
get the matrix data.  unlist() will retain just the atomic data contents 
(stripping off class and attribute data), and will produce nonsensical 
results for S4 classes.  Unless you know that the result that sapply() will 
attempt to simplify is purely atomic data, or if the atomic data is all 
you're interested in, it's usually best to use lapply().

e.g.:

> x <- list(x=factor(letters[1:3],levels=letters[3:1]),y=factor(LETTERS[1:3]))
> x
$x:
[1] "a" "b" "c"
$x: Levels:
[1] "c" "b" "a"

$y:
[1] "A" "B" "C"
$y: Levels:
[1] "A" "B" "C"

> sapply(x, c)
     x y
[1,] 3 1
[2,] 2 2
[3,] 1 3
> sapply(x, as.numeric)
     x y
[1,] 3 1
[2,] 2 2
[3,] 1 3
> sapply(x, codes)
     x y
[1,] 1 1
[2,] 2 2
[3,] 3 3
>
> xx <- list(x=timeSeq(from=1,len=3), y=timeSeq(from=10,len=3))
> xx
$x:
[1] 1960/01/02 1960/01/03 1960/01/04

$y:
[1] 1960/01/11 1960/01/12 1960/01/13

> sapply(xx, concat)
                x            y
[1,] list, 2      %Y/%02m/%02d
[2,] character, 2 GMT
[3,] CLASS, 2     list, 2
>

