Message-ID: <3A822319EB35174CA3714066D590DCD50205CC4D@usrymx25.merck.com>

As Prof. Ripley pointed out, I need to study the lexical scope in R more
carefully.

The scoping rule in Splus, I believe, is the issue here.  If f1() calls f2()
and f2() calls f3(), then objects defined in f2() won't be visible to f3(),
but those in frame 1 will be visible to all on the call stack.  The problem
with formulae is that, users might not be aware that the functions that take
a formula as argument usually calls other functions to process the formula,
and the data frame for which the formula is intended to be evaluated in
might not be visible to those functions.  One cure is to assign the data
frame to frame 1.  Since frame 1 goes away when the top level expression is
finished, it's not exactly "global", as least not as global as frame 0.  The
scoping rule is what it is.  You can try to live with it, or use something
else.

Andy

