provided some script.

> y <- rnorm(10) # sample data set
> x <- y #         a copy thereof 
> fit <- rpart( y ~ x, control = rpart.control( minsplit = 2 , cp = 0) )
> plot(fit)
> text(fit)
> four.classes <- prune( fit , cp =  fit$cp[ 4, "CP" ] ) # for example
> plot(four.classes)
> text(four.classes)
> predict(four.classes)

This was a useful start.  If others are interested in 'natural breaks', I
attach two scripts and the data from T.A. Slocum's 1999 book, Thematic
Cartography and Visualization, which he uses in a discusion of isopleth
boundaries (Chapter 4).  The function, as is, does not exactly reproduce
Slocum's boundaries, but is close.  I continue to work on the problem, but
it was time to post a Summary.

Once again, a thank you to the S-News community.

