From spencer.graves at pdf.com  Mon Oct 13 11:24:33 2003
From: spencer.graves at pdf.com (Spencer Graves)
Date: Mon, 13 Oct 2003 04:24:33 -0700
Subject: Re: Strange behaviour of dbeta
In-Reply-To: <Pine.LNX.4.44.0310131036060.1124-100000@gannet.stats>
References: <Pine.LNX.4.44.0310131036060.1124-100000@gannet.stats>
Message-ID: <3F8A8B71.40005@pdf.com>

1.  Prof. Ripley is correct that the standard definition of a density 
function can exclude any set of probability 0. 

2.  However, the standard definition for the density function for the 
beta distribution is continuous and is the formula you programmed.  It 
is easy to compute your two examples by hand.  One factor in dbeta(x=0, 
shape1=0.35, shape2=3.5) is x^(shape1-1) = 0^(0.35-1) = Inf.  The other 
two factors in the standard definition are finite.  Therefore, R gives 
the "regular" answer, while the S-Plus 6.1 answer is "irregular". 

3.  More serious is the behavior of pt(2, df) as df -> Inf.  This is 
important if I want to estimate the degrees of freedom in Student's t:  
I don't want the algorithm becoming unstable in certain regions.  
Considered "pt(2, 1e8); pt(2, 1e9); pt(2, 1e10); pt(2, 1e20); pt(2, 
Inf); pnorm(2)".  In R 1.7.1 under Windows 2000, these all give 
"0.9772499", to this many significant digits.  With the exception of 
pt(2, 1e20), S-Plus 6.1 shows a gradual degradation in numerical 
precision, producing 0.5 for pt(2, Inf).  With pt(2, 1e20), S-Plus 
attempts a very extensive computation, and I have never had the patience 
to permit it to terminate naturally.  I don't know if it would terminate 
in minutes, hours, or millennia.  I'd call that a bug.  I've learned not 
to trust the probability distribution code in S-Plus without checking;  
the corresponding code in R appears to have been more recently updated 
and extended. 

hope this helps. 
spencer graves

