From: ripley at stats.ox.ac.uk (Prof Brian Ripley)
Date: Mon, 13 Oct 2003 12:56:37 +0100
Subject: Re: Strange behaviour of dbeta
In-Reply-To: <3F8A8B71.40005@pdf.com>
References: <3F8A8B71.40005@pdf.com>
Message-ID: <Pine.LNX.4.44.0310131248240.1452-100000@gannet.stats>

On Mon, 13 Oct 2003, Spencer Graves wrote:

> 1.  Prof. Ripley is correct that the standard definition of a density 
> function can exclude any set of probability 0. 
> 
> 2.  However, the standard definition for the density function for the 
> beta distribution is continuous and is the formula you programmed.  It 
> is easy to compute your two examples by hand.  One factor in dbeta(x=0, 
> shape1=0.35, shape2=3.5) is x^(shape1-1) = 0^(0.35-1) = Inf.  The other 
> two factors in the standard definition are finite.  Therefore, R gives 
> the "regular" answer, while the S-Plus 6.1 answer is "irregular". 

Your defn is *not* continuous:  dbeta(0+) = Inf and dbeta(0-) = 0.
The argument is really about left- vs right-continuity (with one 
convention at 0 and the other one at one): appealing to continuity cannot 
resolve it.

The Encyclopedia of Statistical Sciences agrees with R's help page (but
not R's code) in defining the beta density to be non-zero on 0 < x < 1
(only):  that suggests that the S-PLUS answer of 0 is the better-supported
one.

