David,

  If all you want is some indicator variables based on some factor then 
use contr.treatment. As in:

foo.1 <- factor(rep(letters[1:4], 5))
# drop any unused levels (none in this case)
foo.1 <- foo.1[drop = TRUE]
# scramble things up to make it interesting
foo.1 <- foo.1[sample(length(foo.1))]
# get the levels of foo.1
lev.foo.1 <- levels(foo.1)
# now use contr.treatment and merge
indicator <- contr.treatment(lev.foo.1, contrasts = FALSE)
foo.2 <- merge(data.frame(foo.1 = lev.foo.1, indicator),
               data.frame(foo.1 = foo.1, ord = seq(along = foo.1)))
# merge sorts things in S-PLUS, so re-sort by "ord"
# in R, just use sort = FALSE and drop the "ord" variable
foo.2 <- foo.2[order(foo.2$ord), ]
foo.2$ord <- NULL

Hope this is what you want.

-sundar

