I made a sample Group5 data frame with
   > Group5<-data.frame(id=rep(letters[1:3],each=5))
and then did the following
   > id.Group5 <- levels(Group5$id[drop = TRUE])
   > indicator <- paste( "Group5$X <- sapply(Group5$id, FUN = function(x,X)
if(x == X) 1 else 0, X=X)" )
   > indicator <- parse( text = indicator )[[1]]
   > id.Group5
   [1] "a" "b" "c"
   > indicator
   Group5$X <- sapply(Group5$id, FUN = function(x, X)
   if(x == X) 1 else 0, X = X)
   >  for(i in id.Group5)
      {
           INDICATOR <-  do.call("substitute",
                         list(indicator, list(i = i,
                                X = as.character(id.Group5[i]))))
           print(INDICATOR)
      }
   Group5$X <- sapply(Group5$id, FUN = function(x, X)
   if(x == "") 1 else 0, X = "")
   Group5$X <- sapply(Group5$id, FUN = function(x, X)
   if(x == "") 1 else 0, X = "")
   Group5$X <- sapply(Group5$id, FUN = function(x, X)
   if(x == "") 1 else 0, X = "")

My guess at your data form may well be wrong, but it looks like you expect
id.Group5 to be subscriptable by a character, but it has no names.  Should
your for loop be 'for(i in seq(along=id.Group5))'? Then the expressions you
are evaluating would be

   Group5$X <- sapply(Group5$id, FUN = function(x, X)
   if(x == "a") 1 else 0, X = "a")
   Group5$X <- sapply(Group5$id, FUN = function(x, X)
   if(x == "b") 1 else 0, X = "b")
   Group5$X <- sapply(Group5$id, FUN = function(x, X)
   if(x == "c") 1 else 0, X = "c")

I expect that you would like the Group$X to be Group5$a, Group5$b, etc.
substitute() in R and Splus acts differently here.  In R you get

   > substitute(dataframe$column<-seq(len=nrow(dataframe)),
list(column="Col1"))
   dataframe$Col1 <- seq(len = nrow(dataframe))

while in Splus you get

   > substitute(dataframe$column<-seq(len=nrow(dataframe)),
list(column="Col1"))
   dataframe$column <- seq(len = nrow(dataframe))

Another problem with substitute() is that you have gotten
rid of the use of "X" inside sapply's FUN argument, so
the argument X is not relevant.

I think the whole thing would be easier to understand if
you avoided operating on expressions and using eval.  To
assign something to a column whose name is a variable use
    column <- "a"
    dataframe[,column] <- newvalue
instead of
    column <- "a"
    eval(substitute(dataframe$column<-newvalue), list(column=column))

Then something like

   for(column in id.Group5) {
       Group5[,column] <- sapply(Group5$id, FUN = function(x, X) if(x == X)
1 else 0, X = column)
   }

would do it.

I suspect you want to use
        ifelse(x==X, 1, 0)
instead of
        if(x==X)1 else 0
Then you would get

   > Group5<-data.frame(id=rep(letters[1:3],each=5))
   > id.Group5 <- levels(Group5$id[drop = TRUE])
   > for(column in id.Group5) {
         Group5[,column] <- as.vector(sapply(Group5$id, FUN = function(x, X)
ifelse(x==X, 1, 0), X=column))
   }
   > Group5
      id a b c
    1  a 1 0 0
    2  a 1 0 0
    3  a 1 0 0
    4  a 1 0 0
    5  a 1 0 0
    6  b 0 1 0
    7  b 0 1 0
    8  b 0 1 0
    9  b 0 1 0
   10  b 0 1 0
   11  c 0 0 1
   12  c 0 0 1
   13  c 0 0 1
   14  c 0 0 1
   15  c 0 0 1

Is that what you wanted?

