Subject: Re: how do you get an approximate final hessian from nlminb?
In-Reply-To: <3F8DA2E4.3000003@wfu.edu>
References: <3F8DA2E4.3000003@wfu.edu>
Message-ID: <3F8E0E7E.3040507@pdf.com>

I would suggest using optim in the MASS library with method "L-BFGS-B".

Here's an example:

library(MASS)
f <- function(beta, x, y) sum((y - x %*% beta)^2)
x <- matrix(c(rep(1, 10), 1:10), nr = 10)
y <- x %*% c(-2, 3) + rnorm(10)
# don't really need boundaries, but as an example...
op <- optim(c(0, 0), f, method = "L-BFGS-B",
            lower = c(-5, -5), upper = c(5, 5),
            hessian = TRUE, x = x, y = y)

op$par                                   # final parameter estimates
s2 <- sum((y - x %*% op$par)^2)/8        # dispersion
sqrt(s2 * diag(solve(0.5 * op$hessian))) # standard errors

Compare to

summary(lm.fit(x = x, y = y))$coefficients

Hope this is helpful,

-sundar

