From bauerwo at gmx.net  Fri Oct 17 15:29:05 2003
From: bauerwo at gmx.net (W. Bauer)
Date: Fri, 17 Oct 2003 17:29:05 +0200
Subject: Stacked list
Message-ID: <3F9026E1.11199.1924EE5F@localhost>

Dear all 

I would like to add iteratively elements to a list. These elements are lists again. Yet, 
the c() command does not preserve the structure of the sublists. E.g.

l<-list(1:2)#starts the list

l<-c(l,list(5:6, 3:7))

yields

> l

[[1]]:

[1] 1 2

[[2]]:

[1] 5 6

[[3]]:

[1] 3 4 5 6 7

But I would like my added components to remain lists. How can this be done? Using 
l<-vector("list", ) results in the same problem: when the elements of l are assigned 
lists, these lists loose their structure.

Thanks, Wolfgang Bauer

From tplate at blackmesacapital.com  Fri Oct 17 15:42:36 2003
From: tplate at blackmesacapital.com (Tony Plate)
Date: Fri, 17 Oct 2003 09:42:36 -0600
Subject: Re: Stacked list
In-Reply-To: <3F9026E1.11199.1924EE5F@localhost>
References: <3F9026E1.11199.1924EE5F@localhost>
Message-ID: <5.2.1.1.2.20031017093834.0409f748@mailhost.blackmesacapital.com>

When given list arguments, c() *concatenates* its arguments.  This is the 
intended behavior of c().

To get what you want, wrap your argument to c() in another list, or just 
use list() to assemble the arguments into a list()

i.e.

> l<-c(list(l),list(list(5:6, 3:7)))
OR
> l<-list(l,list(5:6, 3:7))

You also have to be careful with using "[[<-" and "[<-" on lists.  The 
documentation contains some useful information.

hope this helps

-- Tony Plate

